# ics-security-lab — custom Suricata rules (loaded exclusively via `-S`).
# One rule per attack technique. Lab addresses (see docs/architecture.md):
#   PLC (OpenPLC)   172.28.0.10:502
#   simulator       172.28.0.20    the ONLY device that legitimately WRITES
#   HMI             172.28.0.30    reads only
#   attacker        172.28.0.66
#
# Modbus write function codes: FC5 (write single coil), FC6 (write single
# register), FC15/FC16 (write multiple). `modbus:access write` matches them all.
# Rules need the Modbus app-layer parser, enabled in docker-compose.yml with
#   --set app-layer.protocols.modbus.enabled=yes

# 1) Any Modbus WRITE whose source is not the simulator. The simulator is the
#    only host that should ever write this PLC, so a write from anyone else is
#    unauthorized by definition. Catches pump_override and sensor_spoof.
alert modbus !172.28.0.20 any -> 172.28.0.10 502 (msg:"ICS-LAB Unauthorized Modbus write (source is not the simulator)"; flow:to_server,established; modbus:access write; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000001; rev:1; metadata:attack unauthorized_write;)

# 2) A WRITE to holding register 1024 (%MW0, the tank level) from any host other
#    than the simulator — the sensor-spoofing attack specifically. Higher-
#    specificity companion to rule 1; both fire on a level spoof.
alert modbus !172.28.0.20 any -> 172.28.0.10 502 (msg:"ICS-LAB Level register spoof (write to %MW0 / holding 1024 from unauthorized host)"; flow:to_server,established; modbus:access write holding, address 1024; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:1000002; rev:1; metadata:attack sensor_spoof;)

# 3) A burst of writes from one non-simulator host: more than 15 write requests
#    in 2 seconds. Flags rapid override/flooding (sensor_spoof hammers the
#    register ~10x/s); detection_filter only alerts once the rate is exceeded.
alert modbus !172.28.0.20 any -> 172.28.0.10 502 (msg:"ICS-LAB Burst of Modbus writes from one host (override/flood)"; flow:to_server,established; modbus:access write; detection_filter:track by_src, count 15, seconds 2; classtype:attempted-dos; sid:1000003; rev:1; metadata:attack write_burst;)

# 4) A Modbus READ from any host that is neither the simulator nor the HMI. Only
#    those two legitimately read the PLC, so reads from anyone else are recon /
#    register scanning. Catches recon.py enumerating the Modbus tables.
alert modbus ![172.28.0.20,172.28.0.30] any -> 172.28.0.10 502 (msg:"ICS-LAB Register scan (Modbus read from an unexpected host)"; flow:to_server,established; modbus:access read; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:1000004; rev:1; metadata:attack register_scan;)
